{"text": "Emily E. Buckman\n\nProfessional Experience\nOffice of Congressman Brett Guthrie (K Y -02) Washington, DC\nSenior Legislative Assistant/Legislative Assistant (January 2013-Present)\nManage Education and Workforce Development Subcommittee Chairman Guthrie\u2019s legislative portfolio, and serve as\nliaison between committee and member\u2019s staff to achieve the Chairman's objectives and goals\nAdvise the Congressman on key issues including agriculture, education, transportation, trade, tax, small business, labor,\nand judiciary policy\nOversee the Congressional Bourbon Caucus, co-chaired by the Congressman, to promote one of Kentucky\u2019s signature\nindustries\nConduct research and draft memos to prepare the Congressman for committee hearings, meetings with constituents,\ninterest groups, and federal/state/local officials\nCollaborate with staff in other congressional offices and the executive branch on issues important to Kentucky or the\nCongress\nOrganize policy roundtables in the district focused on pending legislation\nKey legislative achievements include:\no Sponsored H.R. 3092, E. Clay Shaw, Jr. Missing Children's Assistance Reauthorization Act (P.L.113-38)\nwhich was signed into law on September 30, 2013\no Introduced H.R. 3179, the Empowering Students Through Enhanced Financial Counseling Act, which passed\nthe House of Representatives on July 11, 2016\no Secured the inclusion of infrastructure language in H.R. 22, the Fixing America\u2019s Surface Transportation Act\n(P.L.114-94) that was crucial to the future economic needs of Western Kentucky citizens and small businesses\no Worked with local stakeholders and Committee staff to secure language in H.R. 5003, the Improving Child\nNutrition and Education Act, to ensure family involvement in the decision making process around child\nnutrition and healthy eating\n\nLegislative Correspondent (January 2011- December 2012)\nManaged all incoming and outgoing mail\nResearched, drafted, and maintained 500+ letter library on various legislative issues important to the Congressman\u2019s\nconstituents\nRegularly updated constituents regarding the Congressman\u2019s work via targeted mass mailings\nDrafted congratulatory letters for constituents\n\nStaff Assistant/Intern (September 2009-December 2010)\n\u00a2 Coordinated constituent tours of the United States Capitol, White House, and other D.C. attractions\ne Managed the technology needs of the Washington, D.C. and district offices\ne Organized the 2010 Congressional Art Competition for Kentucky\u2019s Second Congressional District\n\nKentucky Department of Fish and Wildlife Resources Frankfort, KY\nIntern (Spring 2009)\ne Researched ideas for future policy agendas for the department legislative liaison\ne Drafted reviews on legislation pertinent to the department\n\nEducation\nCampbelisville University\n\nBachelor Oi \u00b0\u00b0 magna cum laude, May 2009\nMajor GPA\n\nVolunteerism and Community Involvement\nEverybody Wins! House Advisory Council Member and Power Lunch Volunteer (2013-Present), JCCIC 58\" Presidential\n\nInauguration Volunteer, Mitch McConnell for Senate Campaign Volunteer (2014/2018), NRCC Rolling Surge Volunteer\n(2012), Vice Presidential Debate Volunteer (2012), Heritage Foundation Congressional Fellow (2011), RNC Volunteer (2010),\nCongressional Institute Leadership Program (2010), NRCC Call to Arms Program (2009)",
